Indian Buddhist philosophers in the Dignāga–Dharmakīrti lineage defend forms of reflexive awareness, while other Buddhist traditions dispute whether such reflexivity exists ultimately or even conventionally. The debate is therefore not simply Śaivism against Buddhism; reflexivity is already an internal Buddhist controversy. ([Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy][1])

Phenomenological approaches likewise distinguish prereflective self-consciousness from reflective introspection or an autobiographical self-representation. ([Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy][2])

