# V. Simulated Checkpoint 6 — Unity may not be an equivalence relation

The enquiry then assumed that experiences belonging to one subject would form cleanly separated classes.

Let:

[
U(x,y)
======

\text{(x) and (y) are co-conscious}.
]

A classical partition requires (U) to be:

1. reflexive;
2. symmetric;
3. transitive.

Transitivity would mean:

[
U(x,y)\land U(y,z)\Rightarrow U(x,z).
]

But split-brain research and philosophy make this assumption unsafe.

Contemporary accounts distinguish one-stream, two-stream and partial-stream interpretations of split-brain consciousness. No simple consensus exists that surgically disconnected subjects always possess exactly one or exactly two conscious streams. ([Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy][6])

A layered model permits:

[
U(x,y),
]

[
U(y,z),
]

but:

[
\neg U(x,z).
]

Recent work explicitly develops a model in which phenomenal unity is non-transitive across local and global experiential layers. ([Springer][7])

Therefore subjects may not be represented by a conventional partition:

[
\Omega/U.
]

They may instead require a hypergraph:

[
\mathcal{H}=(E,\mathcal{U}),
]

where:

* (E) is the set of experiential elements;
* each (U_k\in\mathcal{U}) is a domain of co-manifestation;
* domains may overlap;
* domains may be nested;
* no unique maximal partition is guaranteed.

This produces a major correction:

[
\boxed{
\text{one experience may participate in more than one layer of unity}
}
]

without all layers collapsing into one field.

Consequently:

[
\boxed{
\text{subjecthood may be graded, layered or dynamically reconfigured.}
}
]

This pressures every substance theory.

It also pressures Pratyabhijñā.

If local phenomenal unity can be non-transitive, then saying that all experience rests in consciousness does not show that all experience is co-conscious in one universal field.

Checkpoint 6 closes with:

[
\boxed{
\text{ground unity}
\not\Rightarrow
\text{phenomenal co-unity}.
}
]

