Ratié describes the Pratyabhijñā position as identifying all phenomena, including empirical subjects, as limited aspects of Śiva understood as a single consciousness encompassing the universe. Liberation consists in recognition of one’s identity with this universal consciousness. ([Academia][10])

But universalization had been shown to require distinctions among:

[
\begin{aligned}
G_1&=\text{generic unity};\
G_2&=\text{ground unity};\
G_3&=\text{field unity};\
G_4&=\text{subject unity};\
G_5&=\text{agency unity}.
