The progression is not automatic:

[
G_1\not\Rightarrow G_2
\not\Rightarrow G_3
\not\Rightarrow G_4
\not\Rightarrow G_5.
]

Contemporary cosmopsychism demonstrates the exact problem.

Cosmopsychism places consciousness at the cosmic rather than microphysical level, often partly to avoid the difficulty of combining micro-subjects into macro-subjects. But it then faces the **decombination problem**:

[
\boxed{
\text{How does one cosmic consciousness ground many local subjects?}
}
]

The Stanford Encyclopedia’s discussion notes that cosmopsychism avoids subject-summing only by inheriting the inverse problem: why and how local subjects arise from cosmic consciousness. ([Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy][11])

This is exactly the uncompleted Śaiva problem.

The five kañcukas provide a grammar of finitude:

[
{
\text{restricted knowledge},
\text{restricted action},
\text{need},
\text{sequence},
\text{causal order}
}.
]

But classification is not generation.

To say:

[
C_\infty
\overset{L_i}{\longrightarrow}
C_i
]

does not explain what (L_i) is or why it yields an exclusive local field.

If (L_i) is non-conscious, universal consciousness is limited by something external.

If (L_i) is already an act of universal consciousness, the account risks circularity:

[
\text{Śiva produces limitation through freedom}
]

because:

[
\text{freedom is the power to produce limitation}.
]

If (L_i) is intrinsic to manifestation, then the tradition must establish why manifestation intrinsically possesses a self-partitioning structure.

Checkpoint 8 closes with:

