# VIII. Simulated Checkpoint 9 — Local agreement does not guarantee a global subject

The enquiry next examined whether multiple local fields could be treated as restrictions of one global field.

A useful formal analogy comes from sheaf theory.

A sheaf-like framework asks whether locally defined structures that agree where they overlap can be “glued” into one global structure.

In the Abramsky–Brandenburger treatment of contextuality, locally consistent data need not admit a global section; contextuality corresponds to an obstruction to global gluing. ([arXiv][12])

This mathematical theory does not prove anything about consciousness.

But it supplies an exact formal warning:

[
\boxed{
\text{local coherence does not entail global unifiability.}
}
]

Let each finite subject provide a local phenomenal section:

[
\sigma_i\in F(U_i).
]

Pratyabhijñā requires not only that each (\sigma_i) be manifest.

It requires the existence of a global section:

[
\sigma_\infty
]

such that:

[
\sigma_\infty|_{U_i}=\sigma_i.
]

But informational privacy means that local sections lack ordinary phenomenal overlaps:

[
U_i\cap U_j
\approx
\varnothing
]

at the level of direct co-consciousness.

Therefore local compatibility cannot straightforwardly establish a global experiential section.

Even if all local fields share the same formal character, it does not follow that they are restrictions of one globally experienced field.

The distinction is:

[
\boxed{
\text{common local form}
\neq
\text{existence of a global subject}.
}
]

More strongly:

[
\boxed{
\text{the absence of contradiction among local perspectives}
}
]

does not establish:

[
\boxed{
\text{their simultaneous co-presence to one awareness}.
}
]

This creates a new proof burden.

Pratyabhijñā must show either:

1. that all local phenomenal sections genuinely overlap in a common self-presence;
2. that global identity does not require phenomenal co-presence;
3. or that “universal consciousness” is not one cosmic experience in the ordinary sense.

The third option is the strongest reconstruction.

On it:

[
C_\infty
]

is not a gigantic viewpoint containing every local experience as simultaneously accessible content.

It is:

[
\boxed{
\text{the unrestricted capacity or ground of manifestation}
}
]

from which local fields arise.

But then:

[
C_\infty
\neq
\text{one subject in the same sense as }C_i.
]

Universal consciousness would be transperspectival rather than a universal perspective.

That protects the theory from the absurd implication that the liberated individual should directly access every organism’s memories.

But it weakens numerical identity.

The relation becomes:

[
\text{Ground}(C_i)=C_\infty,
]

not necessarily:

[
\text{Subject}(C_i)=\text{Subject}(C_j).
]

Checkpoint 9 therefore closes with a forced choice:

[
\boxed{
\begin{array}{l}
