# IX. The central theorem of Checkpoint 10

The preceding rounds converge on one theorem.

Let:

[
\Omega
]

represent the total set of potentially manifest contents.

A theory of subjecthood must specify a structure:

[
\Pi:
\Omega
\rightarrow
\mathcal{D},
]

where (\mathcal{D}) is a set or family of phenomenal domains.

For each domain (D_i):

[
D_i\subseteq\Omega.
]

A complete theory must explain:

## Inclusion

Why:

[
x,y\in D_i
]

are co-manifest.

## Exclusion

Why:

[
x\in D_i,\quad z\notin D_i
]

and why (z) is not co-present with (x).

## Scale

Why (D_i) occurs at this organizational scale rather than at cellular, hemispheric, organismic or social scale.

## Temporality

Why the domain persists, divides, merges or changes through time.

## Ownership

Why (D_i) is presented as mine, anonymous or universal.

## Ontology

Whether (D_i) is:

* generated by non-conscious structure;
* identical with a physical structure;
* a local mode of universal manifestation;
* or an irreducible local subject.

No current framework has supplied all six.

Therefore:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{The hard problem is not only the production of experience.}
}
]

It contains a second problem:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{Why does experience occur in these domains?}
}
]

This is the **perspectival partition problem**.

