# X. The law of phenomenal exclusion

The deepest missing principle can now be stated.

A phenomenal domain is not merely a set of represented contents.

It has two-sided structure:

[
D_i
===

\langle
\text{what is co-present},
\text{what is excluded}
\rangle.
]

Its identity partly depends upon what is absent from it.

This resembles the subject-summing objection in contemporary panpsychism: a point of view appears to be characterized not only by the experiences it contains but by its exclusion of other experiences. The combination problem asks how distinct subjects could compose a further subject without violating their apparently exclusive viewpoints. ([Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy][13])

The inverse applies to cosmopsychism and Pratyabhijñā:

> How can an unrestricted field become genuinely local if its local perspectives are constituted by exclusion?

The required law has the form:

[
\Lambda(M,\Sigma)
\rightarrow
{D_1,D_2,\ldots,D_n},
]

where:

* (M) is manifestation or the capacity for manifestation;
* (\Sigma) is the relevant physical, causal or self-articulating organization;
* (\Lambda) establishes domains of co-presence and exclusion.

Naturalism proposes:

[
\Sigma\rightarrow\Lambda\rightarrow D_i.
]

Pratyabhijñā proposes:

[
M\rightarrow\Lambda\rightarrow D_i.
]

Neutral monism proposes:

[
X\rightarrow
\begin{cases}
\Sigma\
D_i
\end{cases}
]

for a more fundamental (X).

But all three leave (\Lambda) incompletely specified.

Thus:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{The common explanatory gap is the law that maps}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\textbf{organization or manifestation into exclusive phenomenal domains.}
}
]

