# XI. The absent-boundary argument has been surpassed

Checkpoint 2’s immediate frontier was:

> Is separation between consciousnesses a positive boundary that must appear, or merely an absence of co-consciousness?

The debate has now produced the answer:

[
\boxed{
\text{phenomenal separation need not appear as a positive boundary.}
}
]

Another person’s experience is not ordinarily present as:

[
\text{“an experience located behind a wall.”}
]

It is simply absent.

Therefore the Śaiva argument:

[
\text{every boundary appears within manifestation}
]

cannot prove that subjects are not genuinely separate.

Phenomenal exclusion may be privative:

[
E(x,z)=\neg U(x,z).
]

It need not be an object or boundary appearing inside either field.

Thus:

[
\boxed{
\text{epistemic inclusion of every represented boundary}
}
]

does not eliminate:

[
\boxed{
\text{unrepresented phenomenal non-co-presence}.
}
]

The universalization proof must therefore move away from boundary introspection.

It must explain why absence of co-presence is compatible with deeper identity.

