# XII. Numerical identity is no longer the best formulation

The statement:

[
S_i=S_j=C_\infty
]

creates unnecessary contradictions because it treats local and universal subjectivity as members of one logical type.

But a local subject is:

[
\text{a determinate domain of co-presence and exclusion}.
]

An unrestricted universal consciousness cannot be one determinate domain among others.

It cannot possess a local exclusion profile without ceasing to be unrestricted.

Therefore:

[
\boxed{
C_\infty
\text{ cannot be a subject in exactly the same sense as }C_i.
}
]

The strongest reconstruction is:

[
\boxed{
C_i
===

\text{a locally exclusive articulation of }C_\infty.
}
]

This means:

[
\text{Ground}(C_i)=C_\infty,
]

while:

[
\text{Perspective}(C_i)\neq\text{Perspective}(C_j).
]

Recognition then need not mean:

> This organism’s perspective is numerically the same perspective as every other organism’s perspective.

It can mean:

> The manifest capacity constituting this perspective is not exhausted by this perspective’s exclusion profile.

Formally:

[
\boxed{
\operatorname{Ground}(D_i)
\neq
D_i.
}
]

Bondage becomes:

[
\boxed{
D_i=\operatorname{Ground}(D_i)
}
]

as a false exhaustive identification.

Recognition becomes:

[
\boxed{
D_i\subsetneq\operatorname{Expression}(C_\infty).
}
]

This preserves Checkpoint 2’s distinction between limitation and bondage:

[
\text{finite perspective}
\neq
\text{exhaustive identity}.
]

It also avoids claiming that liberation must provide direct access to all other phenomenal domains.

This is philosophically stronger, though it may be weaker than the most literal reading of universal numerical subject identity.

