# XIII. The strongest surviving interpretation of the kañcukas

The kañcukas should no longer be treated merely as five restrictions attached to a pre-existing universal observer.

They can be reconstructed as dimensions governing the production of one exclusion profile.

Let:

[
D_i
===

D(K_i,A_i,V_i,T_i,N_i),
]

where:

* (K_i): available discriminations and information;
* (A_i): possible actions;
* (V_i): value and need;
* (T_i): temporal horizon and sequence;
* (N_i): causal and situational constraint.

Then the fivefold structure does not simply reduce capacities.

It establishes:

[
\boxed{
\text{which possibilities can be co-manifest as this world.}
}
]

The kañcukas therefore classify the axes of **perspectival partition**:

[
\begin{aligned}
\textit{vidyā}&\rightarrow\text{epistemic selection};\
\textit{kalā}&\rightarrow\text{action-space selection};\
\textit{rāga}&\rightarrow\text{value-space selection};\
\textit{kāla}&\rightarrow\text{temporal selection};\
\textit{niyati}&\rightarrow\text{causal-position selection}.
\end{aligned}
]

This is stronger than treating them as a poetic parallel to finite agency.

But it remains structural.

It does not prove that universal consciousness generates these restrictions.

It specifies what any proposed contraction mechanism must accomplish.

---

# XIV. Spanda becomes indispensable

The restriction model appears static:

[
C_\infty
\overset{L_i}{\longrightarrow}
C_i.
]

Spanda corrects this.

A phenomenal domain is continuously maintained through dynamic inclusion and exclusion.

At each moment:

[
D_i(t)
]

must preserve enough continuity with:

[
D_i(t-\Delta t)
]

while allowing content to arise and dissolve.

Thus subjecthood is not a sealed container.

It is:

[
\boxed{
\text{a metastable rhythm of integration, differentiation}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\text{and selective availability.}
}
]

The relevant motion is:

[
\text{opening}
\leftrightarrow
\text{closure},
]

[
\text{differentiation}
\leftrightarrow
\text{integration},
]

[
\text{emergence}
\leftrightarrow
\text{withdrawal}.
]

Spanda therefore supplies the dynamic grammar that the kañcuka model lacks.

A subject is not a static restriction of consciousness.

It is:

[
\boxed{
\text{a dynamically renewed contraction–expansion regime.}
}
]

This offers a better structural correspondence with:

* metastability;
* recurrent neural integration;
* active inference;
* dynamic biological boundary maintenance;
* temporally extended consciousness.

But Spanda still does not establish why the dynamic regime is manifest.

Nor does it establish why one universal dynamism underlies all regimes.

It explains how contraction must operate, not why its ground is Śiva.

---

# XV. Krama sharpens the temporal problem

Krama prevents the subject from being treated as a substance persisting underneath change.

A cognitive event is better represented as:

[
\text{emergence}
\rightarrow
\text{stabilization}
\rightarrow
\text{withdrawal}
\rightarrow
\text{transition}.
]

Temporal consciousness research likewise distinguishes competing models of how succession and change are experienced, including momentary, retentional and extended models. No single model is philosophically uncontested. ([Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy][14])

The important result is:

[
\boxed{
\text{phenomenal unity may belong to an organized temporal cycle,}
}
]

not to an unchanging entity outside the cycle.

Let:

[
\Gamma_i
========

{D_i(t)}_{t_0}^{t_n}.
]

Then diachronic subjecthood may consist in structured continuity of transformation:

[
\Gamma_i
]

rather than:

[
S=\text{unchanging bearer}.
]

This strengthens the process alternative to both Nyāya and simplistic Śaiva substantialism.

But it does not answer whether multiple trajectories:

[
\Gamma_i,\Gamma_j
]

are articulations of one transperspectival ground.

---

# XVI. Bhartṛhari shifts unity from substance to constraint

Bhartṛhari provides another alternative.

Sequential elements become intelligible through a whole-forming structure that is not simply the sum of isolated parts.

Applied to phenomenal unity:

[
\text{unity}
]

may be a global constraint on the interpretation and organization of parts rather than a separate owner containing them.

This suggests:

[
U(x,y)
]

obtains when (x) and (y) participate in one whole-forming articulation.

The advantage is that unity becomes relational and semantic rather than substantial.

The limitation is decisive:

[
\text{whole-forming intelligibility}
\not\Rightarrow
\text{phenomenal co-presence}.
]

A text, computer model or biological network may possess holistic organization without its parts necessarily being experienced together.

Bhartṛhari therefore clarifies synthesis but not manifestation.

---

# XVII. Nyāya remains the strongest local-subject control

Nyāya can accept:

* memory;
* recognition;
* agency;
* other minds;
* diachronic continuity;
* phenomenal privacy.

It explains these through multiple enduring selves possessing changing cognitive qualities. Classical Nyāya accounts personhood through an enduring substantial self with properties such as awareness, memory and volition. ([Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy][15])

Its structure is:

[
S_1,S_2,\ldots,S_n.
]

Its advantage is immediate:

[
\neg U(D_i,D_j)
]

is explained because:

[
S_i\neq S_j.
]

Its burden is equally sharp.

The self is posited as a bearer of cognition, but:

[
\boxed{
\text{what makes cognition manifest to an otherwise non-manifest substance?}
}
]

If consciousness is merely a quality that arises in the self, the theory still needs a relation explaining how the bearer becomes a subject of manifestation.

Nyāya explains ownership by placing cognition in a substance.

It does not necessarily explain manifestness.

The control therefore reveals:

[
\boxed{
\text{plural substances solve privacy more readily than manifestation.}
}
]

---

# XVIII. Buddhism remains the strongest anti-universalization control

A Buddhist process theory can accept:

* manifest episodes;
* causal continuity;
* memory construction;
* organism-relative valuation;
* conventional ownership;
* no permanent subject.

Its strongest reply is:

[
\boxed{
\text{The search for a subject beyond the process reifies}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\text{the integrative functions of the process.}
}
]

On this view:

[
D_i
]

exists conventionally as a temporally maintained stream, but no further owner is required.

The Śaiva reply is:

> A causal stream explains why later states contain information from earlier states, but not why the stream is manifest or prereflectively self-present.

The Buddhist reflexivist can answer:

[
\text{each cognition is reflexively manifest}
]

without positing a universal subject.

The Śaiva must then show why local reflexivity is insufficient.

Checkpoint 10 finds that no successful proof has yet done so.

Thus:

[
\boxed{
\text{Buddhist reflexivism remains a complete countermodel}
}
]

to the transition:

[
\text{reflexivity}
\rightarrow
\text{universal subject}.
]

Its own unsolved burden is:

[
\boxed{
\text{why reflexive episodes form these particular domains}
}
]

rather than remaining phenomenally isolated moments.

---

# XIX. Cosmopsychism and Pratyabhijñā now separate

Contemporary cosmopsychism and Pratyabhijñā overlap only at a coarse level.

Both can propose:

[
\text{cosmic or universal consciousness}
\rightarrow
\text{local conscious subjects}.
]

But cosmopsychism need not claim:

* that the cosmos is an agent;
* that it possesses unrestricted freedom;
* that local subjects are numerically identical with it;
* that liberation consists in recognition of that identity.

The minimal cosmopsychist claim is merely that the universe is conscious or that local consciousness is grounded in cosmic consciousness. ([Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy][13])

Pratyabhijñā adds:

[
\boxed{
\text{universal consciousness}
==============================

\text{self-articulating freedom}.
}
]

Thus even if cosmopsychism solved decombination, the Śaiva would still need to derive:

[
\text{cosmic consciousness}
\rightarrow
\text{cosmic agency}.
]

The traditions must not be collapsed.

---

# XX. Vimarśa has now split into seven claims

The word *vimarśa* was previously doing too much work.

It must now be decomposed:

[
\begin{aligned}
V_1&=\text{non-inert manifestness};\
V_2&=\text{prereflective self-presence};\
V_3&=\text{content differentiation};\
V_4&=\text{relational synthesis};\
V_5&=\text{phenomenal inclusion and exclusion};\
V_6&=\text{action-guiding determination};\
V_7&=\text{unrestricted creative freedom}.
\end{aligned}
]

The valid logical discipline is:

[
\boxed{
V_1\not\Rightarrow V_2
\not\Rightarrow V_3
\not\Rightarrow V_4
\not\Rightarrow V_5
\not\Rightarrow V_6
\not\Rightarrow V_7.
}
]

Pratyabhijñā treats these as expressions of one power.

The scientific rival treats them as different functions requiring different mechanisms.

The exact dispute is:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{Does their apparent unity reveal one intrinsic power,}
}
]

or:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{does “vimarśa” metaphysically unify capacities that}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\textbf{are in fact functionally and causally separable?}
}
]

This is now experimentally approachable in part.

If differentiation, integration, access, self-location and action can dissociate, the weaker inference from one to another is confirmed.

But no dissociation can by itself show that manifestness is non-fundamental.

