# XXV. The strongest revised neutral-monist hypothesis

Neutral or Russellian monism proposes that physical science describes relational or dispositional structure while leaving intrinsic nature underdescribed. The intrinsic reality underlying physical organization might account for consciousness. Contemporary Russellian monism is partly motivated by precisely this gap between structural physical description and intrinsic consciousness. ([Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy][13])

Its strongest form here is:

[
X
\rightarrow
\begin{cases}
\Sigma_i&\text{extrinsic or relational aspect};\
D_i&\text{intrinsic or phenomenal aspect}.
\end{cases}
]

This avoids brute emergence.

But it still requires a law determining why:

[
X_i
]

corresponds to one local domain rather than overlapping or cosmic consciousness.

Thus neutral monism relocates the partition problem into the psychophysical mapping:

[
X\rightarrow D_i.
]

It does not remove it.

